WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:06.020
"Getting one hundred and ninety-five countries to agree costs billions and takes forever."

2
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:10.920
No. Getting countries to *implement* things costs billions.

3
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:16.440
Getting them to *sign papers* is surprisingly cheap. Humans love signing papers.

4
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:26.040
It makes them feel important. What Treaties Actually Cost. Non-governmental organization, or N.G.O., campaign budgets are

5
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:32.100
rarely disclosed publicly, because nonprofits guard their finances the way dragons guard gold.

6
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:38.300
But we know enough to establish something surprising: treaty campaigns run on pocket change.

7
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:47.720
The Ottawa Treaty banning landmines took fourteen months. It started with one staff member, Jody Williams, who grew a coalition

8
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:56.220
to over a thousand organizations across sixty countries. Canada committed one hundred million Canadian dollars over ten

9
00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:03.280
years; international donors added one hundred and sixty-nine million dollars in nineteen ninety-seven for mine action.

10
00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:09.600
One hundred and twenty-two states signed. She won the Nobel Peace Prize.

11
00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:18.060
The Cluster Munitions Convention took about two years. A coalition of N.G.O.s backed by a core group of seven governments

12
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:25.020
got ninety-four states to sign and over a million stockpiled munitions destroyed.

13
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.200
The Arms Trade Treaty took a decade but started with just three supporting governments.

14
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:40.300
The Control Arms coalition, led by Oxfam and Amnesty International, published over fifty reports and built support until

15
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:47.500
one hundred and thirty countries signed. The Nuclear Ban Treaty also took a decade of campaigning.

16
00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:56.240
The International Campaign to Abolish Nuclear Weapons, or I.C.A.N., ran the entire operation with five staff in a Geneva

17
00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.920
office, coordinating four hundred and sixty-eight partner organizations.

18
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:06.300
One hundred and twenty-two states voted yes. Another Nobel Peace Prize.

19
00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:13.680
Pattern: One woman with a phone convinced one hundred and twenty-two countries to ban landmines.

20
00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.460
Five people in a Geneva office won a Nobel Prize. The N.G.O.

21
00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.620
costs are a few million dollars per year. Government conference costs add ten to thirty million dollars.

22
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:30.880
Total to get a treaty signed: fifteen to fifty million dollars.

23
00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:36.330
Your species spends more than that on Super Bowl ads for beer.

24
00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:45.460
Inflation-Adjusted to twenty twenty-four Dollars. Using the Bureau of Labor Statistics, or B.L.S., consumer price index,

25
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:53.620
or C.P.I., data: The Ottawa treaty of nineteen ninety-seven had an original cost estimate of fifteen to twenty-five million

26
00:02:53.620 --> 00:03:00.960
dollars, which is thirty to fifty million in twenty twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier of approximately two times.

27
00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:08.520
The Cluster Munitions convention of two thousand and eight had an original cost estimate of fifteen to twenty-five million

28
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:15.260
dollars, which is twenty-three to thirty-eight million in twenty twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier of approximately

29
00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:23.680
one point five times. The Arms Trade Treaty of twenty thirteen had an original cost estimate of twenty to thirty million

30
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:30.600
dollars, which is twenty-seven to forty-one million in twenty twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier of approximately

31
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:39.980
one point three-five times. The Nuclear Ban Treaty of twenty seventeen had an original cost estimate of ten to twenty million

32
00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:46.780
dollars, which is thirteen to twenty-five million in twenty twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier of approximately

33
00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:56.200
one point two-five times. In today's money: Treaty campaigns cost roughly twenty-five to fifty million twenty twenty-four

34
00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:01.020
U.S. dollars. That's less than one percent of the implementation cost.

35
00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:09.400
Banning landmines: about forty million dollars to get the signatures, and over five billion dollars to actually dig them

36
00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:15.820
up. The paperwork is the cheap part. The not-exploding-anymore part is where it gets expensive.

37
00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:24.920
Cost for a one percent Health Treaty. This one touches military budgets, the sacred cow of national sovereignty.

38
00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:32.400
Suggest moving one percent of the explosion money and politicians look at you like you've asked to borrow their favorite

39
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:40.940
child. So budget three to four times what landmines cost. Phase one, Pre-negotiations including coalition building and expert

40
00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:48.640
drafts, takes eighteen to twenty-four months and costs eight to twelve million dollars, funded by philanthropy like Gates

41
00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:57.300
or Open Philanthropy and champion governments. Phase two, the Global Campaign including the platform, three point five percent

42
00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:05.100
consent, and media, takes three to five years and costs forty-five to eighty million dollars, funded by philanthropy and

43
00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:13.400
crowd-funding. Phase three, the Diplomatic Process including conferences and delegate travel, takes eighteen to thirty months

44
00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:19.260
and costs fifteen to twenty-five million dollars, funded by host governments and the U.N.

45
00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:27.500
Trust Fund. Phase four, the Ratification Drive including legal reviews and parliamentary aid, takes two to four years and

46
00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:31.000
costs five to eight million dollars, funded by philanthropy.

47
00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:40.040
Phase five, the Contingency for opposition management, is ongoing and costs ten to fifteen million dollars for strategic

48
00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:46.360
communications and lobbying. Total: eighty-three to one hundred and forty million dollars.

49
00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:52.760
The Math. Signing the treaty: approximately eighty-three million dollars.

50
00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:58.260
What the treaty unlocks: twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year.

51
00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:04.000
Forever. That's over a three hundred to one return on paperwork.

52
00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:14.220
How to Make Governments Sign Things. Politicians move when they see (a) voters demanding it and (b) political cover.

53
00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:23.000
Give them both: First, prove public demand: get three point five percent of the population (two hundred and eighty million

54
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:32.060
people) to say "yes" with verified identity. Harvard researcher Erica Chenoweth found that every nonviolent campaign with

55
00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:37.240
three point five percent plus participation succeeded. That's not activism.

56
00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:46.460
That's a mandate. Two hundred and eighty million verified humans is the kind of number that makes politicians suddenly remember

57
00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:55.480
they work for you. Second, prove it's safe: peer-reviewed modeling showing every nation gains more in health savings than

58
00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:01.480
it loses in military spending. Politicians need cover the way vampires need darkness.

59
00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:08.020
Give them studies to wave around. Timeline to "Governments Can't Ignore This".

60
00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:16.800
With approximately sixty million dollars in seed funding, you can reach "Treaty-Ready" status in two to three years: A platform

61
00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:22.800
with two hundred and eighty million verified supporters (a three point five percent threshold).

62
00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:31.540
Modeling for country-by-country impact analysis. A coalition of one hundred and twenty plus supportive N.G.O.s and five

63
00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:38.260
to ten champion governments. Once these assets exist, formal negotiations become inevitable.

64
00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:44.920
Not because politicians want them. Because ignoring two hundred and eighty million people is the kind of career decision

65
00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:50.000
that leads to "spending more time with family.". You aren't buying a treaty.

66
00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.470
You're buying the machinery that makes the treaty inevitable.

67
00:07:53.980 --> 00:08:01.920
Political Success Probability: Economic Analysis. To calculate expected value, you need to estimate the probability this

68
00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:09.500
actually works. This section documents the rationale for the one percent central estimate (and a twenty-five percent optimistic

69
00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:14.680
scenario ceiling). Yes, the model assumes a ninety-nine percent chance of failure.

70
00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:23.280
It's still a good bet. Stay with me. Even at a one percent probability of success, this intervention's expected cost per

71
00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:32.600
disability-adjusted life year, or D.A.L.Y., of eighteen cents is still five hundred and three times more cost-effective

72
00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:40.320
than malaria bed nets (eighty-nine dollars per D.A.L.Y.). Bed nets are the gold standard of global health interventions.

73
00:08:40.799 --> 00:08:48.120
The political success probability is one percent, with a ninety-five percent confidence interval between zero point one

74
00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:57.540
percent and ten percent. The expected cost per disability-adjusted life year, or D.A.L.Y., is seventeen point seven cents,

75
00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:01.340
ranging from two point nine cents to three dollars and twenty cents.

76
00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:10.340
The cost-effectiveness versus bed nets is five hundred and three times higher, with a range of thirty times to three thousand

77
00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:18.480
times. The bed net cost per D.A.L.Y. is eighty-nine dollars, with a range of seventy-eight to one hundred dollars.

78
00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:29.040
Historical Precedents (The Depressing Table). For the zero point seven percent official development assistance, or O.D.A.,

79
00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:38.540
target, the goal was zero point seven percent of gross national income, or G.N.I., to foreign aid; only about twenty percent

80
00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:44.480
of countries actually did it over fifty-plus years, primarily Scandinavia and Germany.

81
00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:53.800
The Kyoto Protocol set binding emissions targets from nineteen ninety-seven to twenty-twelve; about fifty-five percent of

82
00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:57.800
countries initially complied before a collapse where the U.S.

83
00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:06.640
never ratified and Canada withdrew. The Paris Agreement involved non-binding pledges from twenty fifteen to the present;

84
00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:14.120
about fifteen to twenty-five percent of the one hundred and ninety-five signatory countries are on track, and perhaps only

85
00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:23.360
forty actually meant it. The NATO two percent G.D.P. defense target has reached compliance for about seventy-two percent

86
00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:31.600
of members as of twenty twenty-four; it was only about thirty-two percent until twenty fourteen, when an actual land war

87
00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:36.780
in Europe motivated countries to spend more on killing. The E.U.

88
00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:44.380
Stability Pact has aimed for a three percent deficit and sixty percent debt since nineteen ninety-seven but is routinely

89
00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:48.620
violated, with even France and Germany breaking their own rules.

90
00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:56.420
Pattern: International financial commitments achieve less than twenty-five percent meaningful compliance.

91
00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:03.030
Your species signs things and then doesn't do them. It's like a New Year's resolution, but for geopolitics.

92
00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:10.850
Why a one percent Treaty is Harder Than Most Precedents. Let's be honest about the obstacles.

93
00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:14.900
This is not a chapter written by someone trying to sell you something.

94
00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:23.220
(It is. But at least it's honest about the hard parts.). Touches military budgets: Politicians would rather discuss their

95
00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:32.300
browser history than their defense budget. Requires ongoing annual allocation: Not a one-time signature like banning a weapon.

96
00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:41.660
It's a gym membership for peace. Monthly. Forever. No immediate security threat: Unlike NATO spending increases post-Ukraine.

97
00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:49.540
Hard to motivate governments without an active catastrophe, even though one hundred and fifty thousand deaths per day apparently

98
00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:57.600
doesn't qualify as one. Novel mechanism: Military-to-health reallocation has never been done.

99
00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:05.020
Humans are suspicious of things they haven't tried before, which is odd for a species that also invented bungee jumping.

100
00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:11.030
Coordination problem: Benefits require critical mass. Nobody wants to go first.

101
00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:16.260
Everybody wants to go second. Why a one percent Treaty Might Beat the Odds.

102
00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:21.660
One. Self-funding: Creates health dividends that exceed the "cost."

103
00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:25.440
You make money doing this. That's unusual for moral obligations.

104
00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:32.140
Two. Bipartisan appeal: "Should we cure more diseases?" polls well across every political spectrum.

105
00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:40.320
Shocking, I know. Three. Referendum pathway: Can bypass government resistance through direct democracy.

106
00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:48.380
Politicians hate this one weird trick. Four. Visible beneficiaries: one hundred and fifty thousand deaths per day.

107
00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:55.440
Hard to argue with the body count. Five. Network effects: Each signing country makes the next more likely.

108
00:12:56.020 --> 00:13:00.920
Peer pressure: humanity's most reliable technology. Probability Estimates.

109
00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:08.720
Economists would find the following defensible (and economists find very little defensible): For the pessimistic floor,

110
00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:17.320
the probability is zero point one percent, where everything goes wrong: global crises, political gridlock, or possibly an

111
00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:24.880
asteroid. For the conservative central scenario, there is a one percent probability, representing a ninety-nine percent

112
00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:29.740
chance of failure, yet still five hundred and three times better than bed nets.

113
00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:38.240
For the optimistic ceiling, the probability is twenty-five percent, where a major crisis opens a political window, or humans

114
00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:48.080
just decide to be smart for once. The model uses: Central estimate one percent Expected Value (Even When You Assume Failure).

115
00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:56.660
At one percent probability of success: Expected R.O.I.: eight hundred and forty-eight thousand to one (still positive, even

116
00:13:56.660 --> 00:14:05.260
assuming near-certain failure). Expected cost per disability-adjusted life year, or D.A.L.Y.: seventeen point seven cents

117
00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:10.400
(five hundred and three times better than bed nets at eighty-nine dollars per D.A.L.Y.).

118
00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:16.320
Downside: capped at approximately one billion dollars. You lose the cost of a nice yacht.

119
00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:27.180
Upside: unbounded. You cure most diseases. Even assuming a ninety-nine percent chance of total failure, this beats the gold

120
00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:32.160
standard of global health interventions. The math doesn't care about your pessimism.

121
00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:39.720
Why The Treaty Won't Stop at one percent Most international commitments stagnate after signing.

122
00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:47.740
You saw the depressing table. Zero point seven percent official development assistance, or O.D.A., targets unmet for fifty-plus

123
00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:55.660
years. Kyoto abandoned. Paris missed. Your track record with follow-through makes goldfish look disciplined.

124
00:14:55.740 --> 00:15:01.840
So why would a one percent health treaty be any different? Because this time, people get paid.

125
00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:09.540
The eighty-ten-ten funding structure allocates ten percent of treaty funding (two point seven two billion dollars per year

126
00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:17.560
at one percent) to Incentive Alignment Bonds. These are securities that pay out when treaties expand, not just maintain.

127
00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:22.140
In other words: people get rich when more diseases get cured.

128
00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:25.760
Finally, a financial instrument that isn't morally bankrupt.

129
00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:31.980
The Expansion Mechanism. At a one percent treaty level, the annual I.A.B.

130
00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:39.680
pool is two point seven two billion dollars per year, creating strong political pressure equivalent to ten times the largest

131
00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:47.800
N.G.O. budgets. At a two percent level, the pool is five point four four billion dollars per year, creating very strong

132
00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:54.340
political pressure at a nation-state level. At a five percent level, the pool is thirteen point six billion dollars per

133
00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:58.620
year, creating overwhelming political pressure like a major industry lobby.

134
00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:06.840
At a ten percent level, the pool is twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year, creating unstoppable pressure with

135
00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:16.320
no precedent. Why This Differs from Every Other Treaty. zero point seven percent official development assistance, or O.D.A.,

136
00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:23.560
failed because nobody made money when countries complied. The N.G.O.s pushing for compliance have small budgets, compete

137
00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:28.480
with each other, and run on guilt. Guilt has terrible fuel efficiency.

138
00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:33.180
The one percent health treaty creates its own expansion lobby.

139
00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:39.480
Bond holders profit when the treaty expands to two percent, then five percent, then ten percent.

140
00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:48.060
These aren't activists you can ignore at a dinner party. They're investors with quarterly earnings calls about curing diseases.

141
00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:58.560
The Ratchet Effect. Once one percent passes: Two point seven two billion dollars per year flows to impact acceleration bond,

142
00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:08.579
or I.A.B., holders pushing for two percent. When two percent passes, five point four four billion dollars per year pushes

143
00:17:08.579 --> 00:17:16.319
for five percent. When five percent passes, thirteen point six billion dollars per year pushes for ten percent.

144
00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:21.319
The political pressure for expansion *accelerates* with each success.

145
00:17:22.319 --> 00:17:27.520
Most treaties are gym memberships purchased in January and abandoned by February.

146
00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:33.620
I.A.B.s are the opposite: the constituency for expansion *grows* with each success.

147
00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:43.420
Greed doesn't take holidays. Long-Term Feasibility. With I.A.B.s, the ten percent allocation creates a perpetual political

148
00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:50.740
engine. A machine that runs on greed and outputs health. Embarrassing, but effective.

149
00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:57.340
Without I.A.B.s, this joins Kyoto in the Museum of Things Humanity Promised to Do and Then Didn't.
